(footprint "XL-3030RGBC-WS2812B" (version 20221018) (generator pcbnew)
  (layer "F.Cu")
  (descr "XINGLIGHT XL-3030RGBC-WS2812B / LCSC C5349958. Addressable RGB LED, 3.0 x 3.0 mm, 4 corner pads. TOE EXTENDED +0.50 mm PER PAD, a DELIBERATE departure from the datasheet Recommended Soldering Pattern (0.9 x 0.8 at 2.5 / 2.0 c/c, which this footprint matched exactly before). Pads are now 1.4 x 0.8 at (+/-1.50, +/-1.0): the HEEL is unchanged at x +/-0.80, so nothing under the package moves - only the outer edge, +/-1.70 -> +/-2.20, taking the overhang past the 3.0 mm body from 0.20 to 0.70 mm. WHY: with a 0.20 mm toe the joint sits under the package and forms no inspectable fillet, so optical inspection cannot distinguish a good joint from a cold one or a head-in-pillow - which left a v3.2 LED-chain field failure (2 of 10 halves) undiagnosable. 0.70 mm gives AOI and a loupe a visible meniscus, and gives a fine soldering-iron tip somewhere to land for rework without going under the part. WHY +0.50 AND NOT MORE: the fillet meniscus forms within ~0.2-0.4 mm of the terminal edge, so inspection value saturates by ~0.5 mm and a longer toe shows only bare copper; and paste scales with pad area, so +0.50 mm is +56% solder (a bigger fillet) whereas +1.00 mm would be +111%, enough to float or tilt this light package during reflow and cause the very defect the change exists to detect. A longer COPPER-ONLY toe with the paste aperture held back was considered and rejected: bare copper adjacent to the joint robs solder outward and thins the fillet. Paste therefore follows the copper 1:1. Y IS UNCHANGED (0.8 tall, +/-1.0), so the 0.710 mm clearance to the Kailh socket pad above each LED is untouched; the courtyard grew in x only, +/-1.5 -> +/-2.45. Pin numbering unchanged: 1 VDD, 2 DOUT, 3 VSS, 4 DIN.")
  (attr smd)
